        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why is my Schwinn treadmill not turning on?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Check the power cord at both ends, test the wall outlet with a lamp, and turn on the power switch. If the switch lights but the console stays dark, push in the circuit breaker next to the switch. Schwinn's newer manuals also require a dedicated 15-amp circuit with no GFCI outlet, power strip or extension cord.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What does E1 mean on a Schwinn 810 treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Schwinn's 810 error code guide treats E1 as a speed sensor problem. Unplug the treadmill for 30 seconds and try again; if it returns, the guide checks that the sensor sits about a penny's thickness from the drive pulley and that its wire is not damaged, and then replaces the sensor.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I reset my Schwinn treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Remove the safety key, switch off, unplug for about a minute, then plug in, switch on and replace the key. On the 815, SFT4.1 and SFT4.3, holding STOP for 3 seconds resets the console. The 810 also has a motor control board calibration in its engineering mode, used for E7 and E8 codes.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I get rid of the LUBE BELT message on my Schwinn?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Lubricate the deck with 100% silicone first. Then, on the 815, SFT4.1 and SFT4.3, hold the SPEED UP and STOP buttons together for a few seconds until the message clears. Pressing any key suspends it until after your next workout. On the 810, the LUBRICATE BELT reminder is accepted by pushing any button.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How often should I lubricate a Schwinn treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The 815, SFT4.1 and SFT4.3 manuals say every 3 months or 150 miles. The 810 manual says every 3 months or 25 hours of use, whichever comes first. Use only 100% silicone lubricant; Schwinn says petroleum-based products will damage the machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my Schwinn treadmill belt keep slipping?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually because the belt has stretched or the deck is dry. Lubricate if it is due, then tighten both rear roller bolts evenly: half a turn at a time with the belt running on an 810, or a quarter turn at a time with the belt stopped on the newer models. Do not over-tighten, which wears the belt and motor.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the Schwinn treadmill customer service number?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Schwinn's help centre listed product support on (800) 605-3369 on 25 November 2026: in-warranty Monday to Friday, 8 am to 5 pm Central, and out-of-warranty with longer hours including weekends. Have the model name, serial number and proof of purchase ready.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
